#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid ""
"with a key it checks *your* document; without one there is nothing that "
"is yours, so it checks **every** document in `validation/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid "`check-grants <document>`, `who-grants`, `granted-by`, `granted-with`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid "**no**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid ""
"they verify signed files against your keyring — a question about other "
"people, not about you"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid "`sources`, `check-methodology`, `collect`, `report`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid ""
"they ask what your configuration resolves, what a page says, and what the"
" repository has been vouched for. None of that is done in anybody's name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid "`refresh-links`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:21
msgid ""
"it rewrites a local page's pins. That page is not signed; the documents "
"that pin *it* are, and they are somebody else's"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:60
msgid ""
"So reading what others have vouched for needs no key of your own. That is"
" what lets `check-grants` run in continuous integration, and lets anyone "
"audit a repository they did not write."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:64
msgid ""
"**`gpg` or `gpg2`?** The scripts call `gpg`, and what they need is "
"**GnuPG 2**. Since GnuPG 2.2 the `gpg` command *is* version 2 and `gpg2` "
"survives as an alias for it, so on a current system `gpg --version` "
"reporting `2.x` is all you have to check. Where `gpg` is still GnuPG 1.4,"
" install GnuPG 2 and make `gpg` resolve to it — through your "
"distribution's alternatives mechanism or a `PATH` entry. Pointing the "
"tool at `gpg2` instead is not an option: the name is written into the "
"scripts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:71
msgid ""
"**1. Create a key**, if you do not already have one you want to use for "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:77
msgid ""
"Take the algorithm GnuPG offers — on 2.4 that is **Ed25519**, which is "
"what this project's own validation document is signed with. Give it an "
"expiry you are willing to maintain; step 7 explains why that is close to "
"free. At the user-ID prompt enter a real name and a real email. Both are "
"required: the scripts split the UID on `<…>`, so a key with no email "
"produces a document that fails schema validation, and a key with no name "
"produces a document with nowhere to live."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:83
msgid "The same thing without prompts, for a container or a fresh machine:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:89
msgid ""
"**2. Keep the revocation certificate, and back up the secret key.** GnuPG"
" writes a revocation certificate the moment the key is created and prints"
" where it put it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:96
msgid ""
"That file is the only way to announce that a key must no longer be "
"trusted — and the moment you need it is precisely the moment you can no "
"longer produce one. Copy it somewhere that is not the machine holding the"
" key, and back up the secret half while you are there:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:104
msgid ""
"Losing the key does **not** invalidate grants you have already issued: "
"verification needs only the public half, which by then is in other "
"people's keyrings. What it ends is your ability to issue or amend any, "
"under a name that is already in the repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:108
msgid ""
"**3. Take the key's fingerprint** — the full 40 hex digits, not the short"
" or long key ID:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:116
msgid ""
"This is the detail that catches everyone. The schema requires "
"`who.pubkey_id` to be exactly **40 uppercase hex digits** — a "
"fingerprint. `gpg --list-keys --keyid-format=long`, the incantation most "
"documentation reaches for, prints the 16-digit long ID, and a document "
"built from it is rejected by `check-jsonschema` with a message about a "
"pattern rather than about the key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:122
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**4. Tell the tool which key to use.** The subcommands in the first row "
"of the table above refuse to start without it, and there are three ways "
"to say it — the same three every other setting has "
"({doc}`../configuration/dev-and-validate`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:137
msgid ""
"`packages/freeports_validate/.env.template` is the environment form, "
"ready to copy next to your other per-project settings. Reading it from "
"the configuration file needs the engine installed alongside — `pip "
"install 'freeports-validate[config]'` — because that is the one thing "
"that knows where a configuration file lives."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:142
msgid ""
"**5. Publish the public half**, because a signature nobody can check is a"
" signature nobody reads. Verification is `gpg --verify` against the "
"signer's public key; without it, `check-grants` can confirm that a "
"document is well formed and that its hashes are current, but not that the"
" signature is yours."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:151
msgid ""
"Three places to put it, in increasing order of how little they ask of the"
" person checking:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "Where"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "How"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "What it gives, and what it does not"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "the repository itself"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "commit `ada.pub.asc` beside your document"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid ""
"anyone with the checkout can verify, fetching nothing — but the key "
"travels with the thing it authenticates, so on its own it attests to "
"nothing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "`keys.openpgp.org`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid ""
"`gpg --keyserver hkps://keys.openpgp.org --send-keys <FINGERPRINT>`, then"
" follow the confirmation mail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid ""
"the server verifies the address before the key becomes searchable by "
"email; unconfirmed, it is retrievable by fingerprint only"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "WKD, on a domain you control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid "publish under `.well-known/openpgpkey/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:115
msgid ""
"`gpg --locate-keys ada@example.org` just finds it — the least ceremony "
"for the person checking, and it ties the key to the domain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:159
msgid ""
"Symmetrically, to check someone else's grants you need *their* public key"
" in your keyring — `gpg --import their.pub.asc`, or `gpg --locate-keys "
"their@email`. A repository with several contributors is a repository "
"where each of them has imported the others."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:163
msgid ""
"**6. Certifying a key** — signing somebody else's — states that *you* "
"have satisfied yourself that the key belongs to the person named on it. "
"Do it after confirming the fingerprint through some channel other than "
"the one that handed you the key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:173
msgid ""
"**`freeports-validate` does not consult trust.** `gpg --verify` succeeds "
"for a good signature from a key you have never certified — it prints "
"*\"WARNING: This key is not certified with a trusted signature\"* and "
"exits zero — and `check-grants` duly reports the signature as valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:177
msgid ""
"Certifying keys is therefore for **your** judgement about who a "
"contributor is; it changes nothing the tool decides. The mechanism ties a"
" claim to a key. Tying that key to a person is yours to do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:181
msgid ""
"What the verification step actually distinguishes, then, is narrower than"
" it looks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Situation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`gpg --verify`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "What `check-grants` reports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "good signature, key certified or not"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "success"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "signature valid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "good signature, signer's key has **expired**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "the document changed after it was signed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "failure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "invalid signature"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "the signer's public key is **not in your keyring**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:190
msgid ""
"The last row is the one that misleads, and it is worth knowing before you"
" accuse anyone: a document reported as having an invalid signature is far"
" more often a key you are missing than a document somebody tampered with."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:194
msgid ""
"**7. Expiry, renewal and revocation.** An expiry date is a dead-man's "
"switch rather than a deadline — it limits how long a key stands "
"unattended, and extending it costs one command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:201
msgid ""
"Then re-export and re-publish, since the new expiry is part of the public"
" key. As the table above records, **expiry does not retract anything**: "
"signatures made while the key was valid keep verifying afterwards, so "
"letting a key lapse never silently invalidates grants you already issued."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:205
msgid ""
"Revocation is the other case, and it is a real statement rather than a "
"lapse: this key must not be trusted, because it was lost or compromised. "
"Publish the certificate you saved in step 2 —"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:213
msgid ""
"— and then re-key: your document records the old fingerprint in "
"`who.pubkey_id` and carries a signature made with it. There is no "
"subcommand for changing that, because it is not a routine operation: "
"replace `who.pubkey_id` with the new fingerprint by hand and run "
"`freeports-validate sign-document --update`. Grants signed by a "
"compromised key should be reconsidered, not merely re-signed — the point "
"of the mechanism is that a name stands behind them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:220
msgid "Creating your validation document"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:226
msgid ""
"It fills the template from your key — name, email, fingerprint — resolves"
" the general methodology from your sources and stamps the `version` field"
" with its hash, validates the result against the schema, and writes it to"
" `<repo>/validation/<your_name>.yaml`, the name lowercased with spaces "
"turned into underscores — creating that directory when the repository has"
" none, since `init-format-repo` lays it down with the rest of the "
"skeleton but a repository assembled by hand may not have it. Creating it "
"is announced. A *missing* `validation/` is also what a misresolved "
"repository root looks like, though, so the root has to look like a "
"formats repository first — by the `metadata/formats.csv` `freeports-dev` "
"checks for — and one that does not is named and refused rather than "
"quietly populated. It refuses to overwrite an existing document, and the "
"new one is unsigned:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:248
msgid ""
"Sign it before doing anything else — every other subcommand verifies the "
"existing signature *before* it will modify the document, and an unsigned "
"document is refused:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:255
msgid "Adopting a methodology, then granting files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:257
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"A grant is always *a file, under a methodology*, so the methodology has "
"to be adopted first. Three are published today — `basic check`, `golden "
"standard`, `agreement and good faith` — and "
"{doc}`../../guides/institutional/why-trust-a-grant` says what each one "
"claims. Names are normalised: underscores become spaces and everything is"
" lowercased, so `golden_standard`, `Golden Standard` and `golden "
"standard` are one methodology."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:269
msgid ""
"Adopting a methodology resolves its page and records the hash of that "
"text; granting a file records the file's own hash and its path relative "
"to the repository root. Both re-sign the document immediately, so it is "
"never left in a state where its contents and its signature disagree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:273
msgid ""
"Nothing is written until every file has passed. A grant is one act — the "
"document is modified file by file and signed once at the end — so "
"refusing halfway would leave a document that had been changed and not "
"signed, which is the state `check-grants` reports as *no signature "
"found*, reached by a command doing what it was told."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:278
msgid "To withdraw:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:286
msgid ""
"The last form asks for confirmation, because it removes every grant made "
"under that methodology at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:289
msgid "What a methodology says it covers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:291
msgid ""
"A methodology page may declare which repository paths it applies to, in "
"an ordinary visible section titled `Supported paths`. Each entry is an "
"inline literal with a prose gloss under it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:304
msgid ""
"The gloss is the substance. `tests/formats/` means one thing in a formats"
" repository and another in the engine's own repository, and that "
"ambiguity is resolved by an author writing down which they mean — never "
"by the tool guessing. So nothing here interprets the prose; everything "
"here shows it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:308
msgid ""
"The pattern grammar is deliberately tiny, because these patterns are read"
" by people deciding whether to trust a grant. A pattern that looked like "
"a shell glob but was not one would be worse than no pattern language at "
"all:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Token"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Matches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`*`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "any part of **one** path segment; never crosses a `/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`**`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "zero or more whole segments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "a trailing `/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "the directory and everything under it — the same as appending `**`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "anything else"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "itself, literally"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:319
msgid ""
"There are no character classes, no braces, no negation and no `?`. "
"Patterns are always relative to the repository root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:322
msgid ""
"**A page with no `Supported paths` section supports any path** — the "
"behaviour from before the section existed, and the honest answer for a "
"methodology whose scope genuinely cannot be written as a set of paths. A "
"section that exists and declares *nothing* is read the other way, as a "
"page whose author opened the subject in order to constrain it and then "
"named none; the refusal says so, because that is almost certainly a "
"mistake in the page rather than a methodology covering nothing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:328
msgid "What follows from the declaration is asymmetric on purpose:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Moment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "No section"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Path matches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "No match"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`grant`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "accepted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid ""
"**refused**, printing every pattern with its gloss. Overridable with "
"`--force`, or by answering the question it asks when you are at a "
"terminal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`check-grants`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "silent"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "a **warning**, never a failure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`who-grants`, `granted-by`, `granted-with`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "listed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "listed and marked `(!)`, with one legend line at the end of the run"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:336
msgid ""
"Refusing at grant time is cheap and catches the mistake while its author "
"is standing there. Failing at check time would break a repository over a "
"page somebody else edited, which is not something a repository's own "
"continuous integration should be able to suffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:340
msgid ""
"`--force` has no configuration-file or environment tier, unlike every "
"*setting* in this command. It is not a setting: it is a per-invocation "
"override of a refusal, and written into a configuration file it would "
"permanently disable the very check it overrides, for grants nobody was "
"present to consider. `grant` also refuses when the methodology page "
"cannot be resolved at all — signing under a text this run could not read "
"is exactly what a signature must not mean — and `--force` overrides that "
"too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:347
msgid "What a page pins, and checking it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:349
msgid ""
"A methodology page is prose, and prose cites things: a diagram, a "
"specification, another document. A grant made under that page is in part "
"a claim about what those said, so a page may pin each of them by hash in "
"an ordinary reStructuredText comment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:363
msgid ""
"`..` followed by text that is not a directive is a comment in every "
"reStructuredText parser: it renders as nothing, breaks no build, and "
"reads plainly in the source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:366
msgid ""
"**The pins are not a second trust root.** They are lines of the page, so "
"the page's own `sha256` — the one your document records — already commits"
" to every one of them. Verifying them is an optional *deepening*, never a"
" separate thing to trust:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:376
msgid ""
"`check-methodology` needs no key. It reports the URI the name resolved "
"from, the page's hash, and three counts: **pinned** targets, **unpinned**"
" ones (a count rather than an error — most prose links are incidental) "
"and **stale** pins, which name something the page no longer cites at all."
" A stale pin is a claim left behind by an edit, and only the person who "
"made the edit can say whether the claim went with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:382
msgid ""
"**Following the pins is the default.** It is the question a reader is "
"really asking — not \"does this page still hash the same\" but \"do the "
"things it relies on still say what its author read\" — and it should not "
"be the one you have to know to ask for. It costs one fetch per pinned "
"resource, so there is a way out: `--no-deep` (or `--shallow`) for one "
"run, `validate.deep: false` in the configuration file, "
"`FREEPORTS_VALIDATE_DEEP=0` in the environment. Turning it off is a "
"decision about cost, not a claim that the page is untrustworthy: the "
"page's own hash still matches, and that is what the document recorded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:390
msgid "To re-pin a page you are the author of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:396
msgid ""
"It fetches everything the page cites, rewrites its `.. sha256:` comments "
"in place, and is idempotent — running it twice changes nothing. It "
"refuses a URI, and it refuses a copy sitting inside the resolver's own "
"cache, because editing one of those would produce a text whose hash "
"matches nothing anybody published. When it does write, it says loudly, in"
" red, that the page's own hash has changed and that **every grant made "
"under it is now invalid**. That is the mechanism working, and it is why "
"this is a separate command rather than a step of `update`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:403
msgid ""
"Two rules govern what it keeps. A pin whose target the run could not "
"reach keeps exactly the hash it had — turning a network failure into a "
"withdrawn claim would be the quietest possible way to lose one. A pin "
"naming something the page has stopped citing is dropped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:407
msgid "Checking grants"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:413
msgid ""
"It reports, item by item: the schema, the signature, the general-"
"methodology version, the hash of every adopted methodology, and the hash "
"of every granted file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:416
msgid ""
"A run ends in one of **three** states, and the exit status distinguishes "
"all three. Two would not be enough, because \"everything I checked was "
"fine\" and \"I checked everything and it was fine\" are different "
"sentences, and only the second is a grant verified:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Exit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "What it means"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`0`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "every claim was compared against what it names, and every comparison held"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`1`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid ""
"something was compared and did not hold: a signature, a version, a "
"methodology hash, a file hash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "`3`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "nothing was found wrong, and not everything was looked at"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:426
msgid ""
"The third is the one worth dwelling on. A methodology page that could not"
" be fetched leaves every grant made under it **uncompared** — neither "
"confirmed nor disproved. Counting that as a failure would accuse a "
"granter over a network outage, so it is not counted; reporting it as a "
"pass would launder \"I could not look\" into \"I looked and it was "
"fine\", which is the one sentence a validation tool must never say. So "
"the run says, in as many words, that those grants are *not vouched for by"
" this run* and should be treated as ungranted until a run that could read"
" the pages says otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:433
msgid ""
"`--offline` makes that state deliberate rather than accidental: it "
"answers from the cache where it can, and only what it could not answer "
"leaves the run inconclusive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:436
msgid "One thing it says is a warning and never any of the three:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "a file granted outside the paths its methodology declares"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid ""
"a warning. Failing here would break a repository over a page somebody "
"else edited"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:442
msgid ""
"The explanations below are printed on a **green** run too: a warning "
"nobody explains is a warning nobody acts on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:445
msgid ""
"Those lines name a discrepancy rather than explain one, so a failing run "
"closes with a short section saying what each *kind* of failure it hit can"
" mean — that an invalid signature is far more often a public key missing "
"from your keyring than a document somebody tampered with, that a file "
"hash mismatch is the mechanism working rather than breaking, that a "
"changed methodology page invalidates the claims made under it because "
"they were claims about that text. For the two hash mismatches that "
"involve a resolved page — a methodology's, and the general methodology "
"behind `version` — the first cause it names is a **differently-configured"
" source**, ahead of the page having changed, and it prints the sources in"
" use and the URI the name resolved to here, so you can compare them with "
"the granter's. It is printed once for the whole run rather than once per "
"document, and only for the kinds that actually occurred; a run with "
"nothing wrong prints none of it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:457
msgid ""
"With no argument, what it looks at depends on whether a key is configured"
" — because that is what decides whether any document here is *yours*:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "Bare `check-grants` checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "a key is configured"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "**only your own** document, the one belonging to that key"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "no key is configured"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:172
msgid "**every** `validation/*.yaml` in the repository"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:466
msgid ""
"The first row has a trap in it. If you have a key configured but no "
"document of your own in this repository, `check-grants` says so and then "
"reports *\"All validation documents passed verification\"* — having "
"verified nothing, because there was nothing of yours to verify. A green "
"bare `check-grants` from a contributor's own machine is not evidence that"
" a repository's grants hold."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:471
msgid ""
"The second row is the form to use in continuous integration, and it needs"
" no key precisely because nothing there speaks in anyone's name: it "
"verifies every document against the keyring it is given."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:475
msgid ""
"A failing signature on a document that is not yours usually means its "
"author's public key is not in your keyring, not that the document was "
"tampered with. Import it before concluding anything: `gpg --import "
"their.pub.asc`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:479
msgid ""
"It answers one narrow question — *are these grants still about these "
"files* — and it is not a substitute for the tests passing, any more than "
"the tests are a substitute for it. One says the code still does what it "
"did yesterday; the other says a person put their name to the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:483
msgid "Asking who stands behind what"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "Command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "Lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "Grouped by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "Alternative"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "`who-grants <files…>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "who vouched for these files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "contributor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "`-m`, by methodology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "`granted-by <contributor>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "what this person vouched for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "methodology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "`-f`, by file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "`granted-with <methodology>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "what was vouched for under this methodology"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:465
msgid "`-c`, by contributor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:491
msgid ""
"A contributor can be named by their name, their email or their key "
"fingerprint — whichever you have."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:493
msgid ""
"All three annotate a grant made outside the paths its methodology "
"declares with `(!)`, and print one legend line at the end of the run "
"explaining the mark. Such a grant is listed rather than hidden: somebody "
"made that claim, and the reader is told under what."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:497
msgid "After a file legitimately changes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:499
msgid ""
"A grant is a claim about *bytes*. When granted content changes for a good"
" reason — a format was fixed, and its reference output genuinely should "
"differ — the grant does not follow it automatically, and that is the "
"design working. Restating it is explicit:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:509
msgid ""
"`update file` restates an existing claim; it does not create one. A file "
"that was never granted under that methodology is refused, naming the "
"`grant` that would be the right command, rather than being accepted, "
"doing nothing and reporting success."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:513
msgid ""
"Each re-signs afterwards. `update methodology` is the heavy one: a "
"changed methodology page means the claims made under it were made about a"
" text that no longer exists, so it **drops every file granted under that "
"methodology**, after asking. That is deliberate — the alternative would "
"be carrying old claims forward under a new meaning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:519
msgid ""
"`update` is not the way to silence a failing `check-grants`. It re-states"
" an intention to vouch, and it should be run by the person who has "
"actually confirmed that the change was expected. Running it because a "
"check went red converts a real signal into a signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:524
msgid "Where the validation files live"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "Path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "What"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "`<repo>/validation/<name>.yaml`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "one validation document per contributor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "`<repo>/package.yaml` → `info.validation_sha256`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "ties the repository to the document vouching for it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "the installed package's `lib/document.schema.json`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid "the schema every document is checked against"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
#, python-brace-format
msgid "`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/freeports-validate/`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:518
msgid ""
"every methodology body ever fetched, keyed by the URI's hash and then by "
"its own"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/cli/freeports-validate.md:532
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The methodology pages are **not** in that list, and that is the point. "
"They ship neither with the repository nor with the tool: they are "
"resolved from the sources you configure, so the text a grant refers to is"
" a document at an address its granter chose. The pages this project "
"publishes are in {doc}`the validation section <../../validation/index>` "
"of this site, which is exactly what the default source fetches — and, "
"being hashed artefacts, they cannot be edited as ordinary prose."
msgstr ""

